WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:08.320
Now we had all the ingredients; we had purified microtubules 

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.280
made from purified tubulin,

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.610
we had the membrane organelles,

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:17.560
we had the chemical energy source ATP, and now we can add them in 

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:22.500
various combinations and see what might work to allow 

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:27.710
this magical process of organelle transport to occur in the test tube.

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:30.730
We found that when we added organelles,

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:35.450
microtubules and ATP, surprisingly, 

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:37.210
there was no movement.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:42.050
But we found that the organelles themselves were not capable of moving 

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:46.950
until we added this soluble protein fraction.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:52.960
And finally we saw tremendous movement of these 

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:54.650
organelles along the microtubules.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:57.120
This was a really exciting moment.

00:00:57.130 --> 00:01:01.750
I had been working essentially for four months to get these organelles to 

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.860
undergo motility in a test tube,

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:05.340
just like in the native axon.

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:08.960
But the next experiment was even more surprising.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.550
It was called a control experiment.

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:17.400
Essentially we wanted to make sure that what we thought we saw moving were in fact the 

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.780
membrane organelles and not something else that might have been 

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.750
coming along with the soluble protein fraction.

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:31.290
So the way to figure that out was just to mix everything again but leave out the 

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:35.460
membrane organelles, just add together the microtubules,

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:40.350
the ATP and the soluble protein fraction. And we thought nothing would happen,

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:42.560
that we wouldn't see anything,

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.250
and then we can call it a night and go home.

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:49.350
But what happened was really mind-blowing.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:53.660
We looked under the microscope and the microtubules,

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:56.490
which are these long biological polymers,

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:58.400
were sliding around,

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.240
crawling around like snakes on the glass surface.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:06.030
That was something that we certainly didn't expect at all.

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:07.500
And in fact,

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:12.070
it even it took us a few minutes to even think about what was happening 

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:12.530
here.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:17.530
Why were these microtubules crawling along the glass surface? After we had 

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:21.300
overcome our amazement and surprise by that result,

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:25.280
we then began to piece together in our head what might be happening.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:31.230
Maybe that soluble protein fraction that contained some agent 

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:36.450
that was causing the organelles to move actually contained the motor 

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:37.330
itself.

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:43.530
So the soluble protein fraction had motors that were floating around 

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:48.470
in it without the organelles and these motors 

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.450
would also be able to stick onto the surface of this glass 

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:54.130
slide,

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:59.210
grab hold of the microtubules and transport these microtubules along the 

00:02:59.210 --> 00:02:59.870
glass.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:01.400
Think of a rock concert;

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:06.730
the singer jumps off the stage into the mosh pit, 

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:12.030
and the people in the audience, who are stationary there, grab hold of the singer 

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:16.440
and transport the rock star across.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:22.720
We thought a very similar thing was happening there with the microtubules crawling on 

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:23.360
glass.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:24.530
The next day,

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:29.630
I was able extend those results to find that 

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:34.900
we could get plastic beads, in the presence of this soluble 

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.920
axoplasmamic protein fraction, also to move along microtubules.

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:42.890
And this is sometimes what happens in science,

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:43.170
you know,

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:44.430
you work really hard,

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:46.610
you don't get any results,

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:49.320
it gets frustrating,

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:50.630
you have to persevere.

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:55.320
And then one magical day,

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:59.700
everything really seems to come together and the 

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:04.345
experiment is suddenly propelled much farther than you could have ever imagined.

00:04:05.045 --> 00:04:07.925
And that's a little bit the nature of science.

00:04:08.815 --> 00:04:11.925
It's when persistence counts for a lot,

00:04:12.535 --> 00:04:15.525
one has to be prepared for the unexpected,

00:04:16.885 --> 00:04:21.585
and one has to believe that one's experiment is going to work in the very 

00:04:21.585 --> 00:04:21.975
end.

00:04:22.565 --> 00:04:26.455
And I think once one has that kind of experience,

00:04:27.225 --> 00:04:31.675
one's also willing to persevere on a lot of other experiments later 

00:04:32.065 --> 00:04:34.025
in one's scientific career as well. 

